1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,567
These captions tell you what happens
in the music, like someone beside you.

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:12,800
The Hero: horns and cellos together.
The theme climbs over three octaves.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Red is the Hero's colour. The
brushstroke appears when the horns sing.

4
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,133
All eight horns strike together.
That is why there are eight knights.

5
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:27,800
The violins only enter now:
the white lines of the wind.

6
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,787
Brass and timpani for the first
time. The brass is the sun.

7
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,073
The dust at their feet is the timpani:
its strokes raise puffs of it.

8
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,900
Woodwinds and harps enter.
This is the Hero's imagination.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:00,233
The terns are the woodwinds.
Every instrument has its own character.

10
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,767
The music grows towards
its first summit.

11
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,200
The Hero's tender side.
The harps ripple in the water.

12
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,933
Two horns sing: two red strokes.
He has dissolved into ink.

13
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:11,233
Strength gathers from below. He is a
knight again, and the troop marches.

14
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:25,233
Now all eight horns play
the theme at full strength.

15
00:02:38,445 --> 00:02:44,445
E flat, as in the Eroica. Strauss promised
no funeral march, but plenty of horns.

16
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,367
Full orchestra, fortissimo.
Ahead lies the storm mountain.

17
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,733
Harps and woodwinds cascade
downwards: the waterfall.

18
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:37,167
Four times the chord breaks off and
a blow falls. Lightning, then onward.

19
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:53,167
One huge chord that refuses
to resolve. The clouds part.

20
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:58,733
The halo is the record's sound:
each ray is one band of frequencies.

21
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,733
The mosquito is the flute,
first critic: "very sharp and pointed".

22
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:11,333
The duck is the oboe.
It mutters, rasping.

23
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:16,433
Two snails, two tubas a fifth apart:
Doktor Dehring, the pedant.

24
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:21,633
Parallel fifths break his own rules.
Listen: Dok-tor Deh-ring.

25
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:26,967
The heron is the cor anglais,
mocking his theme. The frogs are bassoons.

26
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:30,500
The crows are three muted trombones.

27
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:36,700
All at once. Murk descends:
this is a fight for clarity.

28
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:48,467
The Hero's theme now sounds
in the minor, quiet and dark.

29
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:10,400
The violins rise in waves. He looks
into the pond and remembers the dawn.

30
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:28,540
The memory grows wave by wave,
with the strings.

31
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:53,407
The morning's sun and banners,
upside down in the water.

32
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:09,233
The critics attack again.

33
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:24,400
All eight horns answer.
His comrades have arrived.

34
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:35,400
His patience runs out:
the banner rises and flares.

35
00:06:47,900 --> 00:06:52,033
He drives them off and breaks
into a short hymn of victory.

36
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:06,833
The solo violin is the Companion:
a white line, where no ink is printed.

37
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:11,483
The line rises and falls
with the violin's notes.

38
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:18,533
The horns reply. The Hero speaks
briefly, the violin at length.

39
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:34,200
The Companion is Pauline
de Ahna, Strauss's wife.

40
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:45,033
Strauss called her complex,
a little coquettish, never the same twice.

41
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:55,200
Cellos and basses:
the Hero's second reply.

42
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:42,633
The violin changes mood within
a few bars, like a chameleon.

43
00:08:55,100 --> 00:09:00,100
Strauss wrote the moods into the score:
longing, frivolity, high spirits.

44
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:25,400
The shape of the line is its
mood: arcs, loops, curls.

45
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:55,723
The sharp zigzag is sarcasm.

46
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:09,533
Horns and basses, emphatically:
the Hero tries to win her over.

47
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,333
The violin speeds up,
whirls around him and escapes.

48
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:56,333
The woodwinds return.
An echo of the critics?

49
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:32,533
He flares up: his grandest gesture,
and it is the wrong one.

50
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:44,700
The violin laughs at him, fast and high.

51
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:20,467
The turning point: he stops performing.
A tender, calming phrase.

52
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:34,567
The line comes closer, turn by turn.

53
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:26,167
The love scene, in G flat major:
the whole orchestra sings.

54
00:13:26,367 --> 00:13:30,900
The harps' glissandos:
the moon's path shimmers on the water.

55
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:43,943
For a while, the line takes human form.

56
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:14,533
Red and white entwine,
as their themes do in the music.

57
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:29,040
This is the slow movement:
the whole work is one vast sonata form.

58
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:48,867
Strings and harp begin to climb.

59
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:11,833
Woodwinds break in:
the adversaries are knocking at the door.

60
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:23,433
The peak of love:
the Hero's theme returns.

61
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:29,200
Purple: the first new
colour in the whole video.

62
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:38,883
For Goethe it was the highest colour:
where red and blue meet.

63
00:16:19,700 --> 00:16:23,433
Afterglow. Soft horn calls in the night.

64
00:16:45,728 --> 00:16:50,528
Two figures watch the moon,
as in a painting by Friedrich.

65
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:21,873
Strauss wrote this work
in about three months in 1898.

66
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:42,153
Its sister work is Don Quixote: a
ridiculous hero mirroring this noble one.

67
00:17:56,100 --> 00:18:01,167
Woodwinds whisper: fragments of the
critics in the night's silence.

68
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:26,367
Almost silence. The ink dries
and the night fades to paper.

69
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:39,587
A sketch appears on the
paper: what is to come.

70
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:50,067
Three trumpets offstage call
to battle: three banners.

71
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:08,433
The orchestra answers,
and the Hero mounts his horse.

72
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,467
A second call, closer now.

73
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:34,400
Timpani: the battle begins.

74
00:19:46,024 --> 00:19:51,524
To a critic of 1899 this was the
culmination of all ugliness and din.

75
00:19:53,704 --> 00:19:58,237
The white sash: the Companion
rides with him into battle.

76
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:08,320
The critics return as whirlwinds.
Lightning and ink mark written accents.

77
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:28,600
Side A ends mid-battle. The field
winds itself into the record's groove.

78
00:20:30,246 --> 00:20:35,513
The sound was once pressed into vinyl.
This video is a new printing of it.

79
00:20:35,713 --> 00:20:38,179
The battle continues on Side B.

